# Real Receipt OCR Value Extraction Plan

Implement client-side OCR using the **Tesseract.js** library loaded dynamically from a CDN. This allows the application to extract actual text content from uploaded receipt image files (like PNG, JPG) to parse the vendor, amount, date, and tax ID, instead of relying solely on filename-based regex or static mock values.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> - **Zero Dependency Installation**: Because local group policies restrict running terminal commands for dependency installation, we will load `Tesseract.js` dynamically from the jsDelivr CDN (`https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/tesseract.js@5.0.3/dist/tesseract.min.js`) at runtime.
> - **OCR Fallback**: If the network is offline or the CDN fails to load, or if a non-image file (like PDF) is uploaded, the app will gracefully fall back to the existing filename-based regex parser (`extractOcrFromFile`).
> - **OCR Parsing Strategy**:
>   - **Amount**: Scan lines for total-related keywords (e.g., `Total`, `Amount`, `Sum`, `Due`) and extract the largest decimal number. If no keywords are found, default to the largest decimal number found below 10,000.
>   - **Date**: Scan lines for standard date formats (`YYYY-MM-DD`, `DD-MM-YYYY`, `MM-DD-YYYY`, and spelled-out months like `Jan 15, 2026`).
>   - **Vendor**: Search text for common known vendors (e.g., `Uber`, `Starbucks`, `McDonald's`, `Walmart`, `Delta Air`, etc.). If none match, extract the first non-trivial line from the receipt header, avoiding noise like "INVOICE" or "RECEIPT".
>   - **Tax ID**: Scan lines for keywords like `Tax ID`, `EIN`, `VAT`, or `GST` followed by alphanumeric codes.

